# Patch: Kimchi host integrations for pi-mcp-adapter 5.3.1
#
# Tracking:
#   - Project config directory: https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter/issues/491
#     (remove when upstream supports a project config directory independent of
#     the user config directory)
#   - Tool-name sanitization and selector matching: getkimchi/kimchi#1168
#     (remove when upstream sanitizes both name parts and caps wire names at
#     the 128-char provider limit)
#   - MCP UI standard disable: no upstream option; remove when the harness
#     supports MCP Apps or upstream adds a disable option
#   - OAuth loopback capture: getkimchi/kimchi#1284; upstream tracking issue
#     pending at https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter (remove when
#     upstream supports an opportunistic loopback listener for manual
#     redirect flows)
#   - OAuth metadata Accept header: strict gateways answer 406 when
#     text/event-stream is missing; upstream issue pending at
#     https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter (remove when upstream
#     sends "Accept: application/json, text/event-stream")
#   - DCR clientInfo version: still hardcoded "2.11.0" in upstream
#     mcp-auth-flow; remove when upstream derives it from the package version
#   - Stored redirect URI reuse: upstream 5.3.1 defaults the loopback host to
#     127.0.0.1 (PR #712), so re-auth of a pre-registered client (e.g. Google)
#     whose registration was created with a localhost URI now sends a different
#     redirect and fails with redirect_uri_mismatch. Fixed-client servers with
#     no usable stored registration (first auth, or a registration whose
#     redirectUris were dropped) now default to the localhost callback adapter
#     2.x sent, since such clients register localhost only; upstream issue
#     pending at https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter (remove when
#     upstream prefers the stored registration's redirect URIs and defaults
#     fixed-client servers to the localhost callback)
#   - Stored redirect URI preservation: mcp-oauth-provider clientInformation()
#     rewrites the stored clientInfo for config-registered clients (issuer
#     binding bookkeeping) and drops its redirectUris, destroying the legacy
#     registration before re-auth can reuse it; same upstream tracking as above
#   - Authorize-URL hook: upstream has no first-class way for a host to add
#     offline-access request parameters (OIDC offline_access scope, vendor
#     params such as Google's access_type=offline) without passing a
#     programmatic config, which disables the /mcp panel and the config write
#     flows; upstream issue pending at
#     https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter (remove when upstream
#     supports offline-access defaults or an authorize-URL customization hook)
#   - Runtime config reload (reload.ts reloadConfig; metadata-cache
#     evictServerMetadataCache; state reload-context fields; index.ts
#     reload-handle sink): upstream 5.3.1 has no runtime config reload API —
#     McpLifecycleManager exposes only reconnect/health/keepalive/idle, and
#     config is read once per install/session replacement. Upstream tracking
#     issue pending at https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter (remove
#     when upstream ships a runtime reload (re-read → diff → reconcile incl.
#     deferred-catalog eviction, trust re-resolution, and tool-surface sync)
#     plus a per-extension-instance callback to register a reload handle).
# Changes:
#   - agent-dir/config (+dist mirrors): resolve the MCP project config
#     directory via piConfig.mcpProjectConfigDir so project MCP config can
#     live outside the user config directory; covers both the adapter config
#     path (mcp-adapter.json) and the legacy pi-mcp compat read (mcp.json).
#   - types (+dist mirrors): sanitize MCP tool names to ^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,128}$
#     (truncating with a warning), normalize selector patterns on both sides,
#     and keep pre-sanitization spellings matching for include/excludeTools.
#   - metadata-cache/server-manager/tool-metadata (+dist mirrors): do not
#     advertise the io.modelcontextprotocol/ui extension capability and do
#     not persist or reconstruct UI resource metadata. (Tool-annotation
#     caching is native upstream as of 5.x, so the 2.34.0-era hunk that
#     persisted annotations in the metadata cache was dropped.)
#   - mcp-auth-flow: manual (remote https) OAuth redirect flows also start an
#     opportunistic loopback listener on the default callback port/path and
#     race it against the manual paste prompt; graceful fallback to
#     paste-only when the listener cannot bind. startAuth reports whether the
#     capture is armed, and non-interactive callers (no onAuthorizationInput,
#     e.g. ACP/Studio) may proceed without a manual paste when it is.
#   - proxy-modes: auth-start registers the background auth watcher for
#     remote https redirects when the loopback capture is armed, and the
#     auth-start instructions reflect automatic completion.
#   - mcp-callback-server (+mcp-auth-flow): callbacks whose oauth state belongs
#     to a recently completed flow replay the normal success page (HTTP 200)
#     via a process-level tombstone (10 min TTL, capped at 100, lazily pruned)
#     recorded only in completeAuth after a successful token exchange (a
#     failed exchange no longer leaves a success-page tombstone); unknown,
#     superseded, or expired states get a neutral "Authorization No Longer
#     Active" error page (HTTP 400) instead of the CSRF error page, with the
#     heading string unchanged so host branding keeps mapping it.
#   - mcp-auth-flow: concurrent token exchanges of the same pending flow
#     (callback listener racing a pasted redirect URL) are serialized via an
#     in-flight map; the second caller awaits and shares the first result,
#     and a paste for an already-completed state returns "authenticated"
#     instead of throwing "No pending OAuth flow".
#   - proxy-modes: a background auth watcher that fails with the callback
#     timeout appends the actionable manual-auth instructions (URL and
#     auth-complete paste command) to the failure status message.
#   - mcp-auth-flow: when the server is configured with a fixed oauth.clientId
#     and no explicit redirectUri, startAuth reuses the stored client
#     registration's first redirect URI (only when the stored registration's
#     clientId matches the configured one) instead of the current default
#     host; unreadable or non-matching stored state falls back to
#     http://localhost:<configured callback port>/callback. Dynamic-registration
#     servers (no clientId) keep upstream's 127.0.0.1 default.
#     See the stored-redirect-URI-reuse tracking entry above.
#   - mcp-oauth-provider: clientInformation() preserves the stored client
#     registration's redirectUris when it rewrites the entry for a
#     config-registered client. See the stored-redirect-URI-preservation
#     tracking entry above.
#   - mcp-auth-flow: DCR clientInfo version bumped from stale hardcoded
#     "2.11.0" to "5.3.1".
#   - mcp-oauth-provider: configured-discovery metadata fetch sends Accept
#     "application/json, text/event-stream" instead of "application/json".
#   - project-server-trust: KIMCHI_MANAGES_MCP_PROJECT_TRUST=1 skips the
#     interactive per-server project approval (host already gates project trust),
#     avoiding a double prompt in the TUI; no approval-store writes occur.
#   - config (+dist mirror): the repointed legacy pi-mcp compat layers stay
#     authoritative and adapter-format in the host topology — the global
#     mcp.json behaves as the user-scope layer and the project mcp.json as
#     the top project layer (each reads/writes itself, outranking shared
#     imports a la 2.34). Pi-style compat parsing (which drops top-level
#     settings/imports and per-server adapter keys) is disabled for these
#     paths, and a project entry MERGES field-wise with the global entry
#     (via mergeServerMaps' credential stripping) instead of replacing it.
#   - Always register /mcp (index.ts): pi 0.99+/1.2 ships a built-in native
#     MCP extension; Kimchi's compat layers own these mcp.json files, so
#     keeping the built-in double-connects servers and its stricter native
#     auth schema ({provider} bearer) warns on adapter-format configs.
#     Registering /mcp at load makes pi's loader omit the replaceable
#     built-in extension (upstream-designed replacement mechanism).
#   - pi-builtin-mcp: ADAPTER_VERSION read is bundled-runtime-safe: under
#     Bun single-executable builds import.meta.url has no real package.json
#     ($bunfs), and the top-level readFileSync threw at import time, so the
#     built-in MCP was never disabled in compiled binaries (double MCP
#     stacks, strict-auth-schema warnings). Falls back to "unknown"; the
#     onboarding-state gate then simply re-checks each start.
#   - config: writeProjectServerDisabledOverride writes its override through
#     the server's provenance path (the file it was loaded from), falling
#     back to the adapter-native project config for unknown servers.
#   - mcp-output-guard (+dist mirrors): default output budget 50 KB -> 12 KB
#     (DEFAULT_MCP_OUTPUT_MAX_BYTES) so oversized MCP results no longer blow
#     the session context on parallel tool results; still overridable via
#     settings.mcpToolOutputTuning.maxBytes.
#   - mcp-oauth-provider (+dist mirror), oauth: setAuthorizationUrlTransform()
#     hook, applied in redirectToAuthorization to the authorize URL (with the
#     flow's saved discovery state) after the server's own
#     authorizationParams, so a host only fills in what config left unset; default is identity. The
#     host (src/extensions/mcp/oauth-offline-access.ts) supplies the policy.
#   - reload/config-state (+dist mirrors): runtime config reload driver
#     (re-read → computeServerHash diff → per-server reconcile), metadata-cache
#     eviction helper, reload-context state fields, and a reload-handle sink
#     option so hosts can reconcile per live extension instance. Review pass:
#     runtime-registered servers (pi.registerMcpServer / the built-in gateway)
#     are mirrored onto each state (runtimeServerNames) and excluded from the
#     removal diff — their runtimeServers entry outlives the reload and a
#     teardown was unrecoverable in-session; the blocked reconcile path now
#     re-syncs the tool surface (notifyToolMetadataUpdated) like remove/disable.
